  "lead": "<img class=\"source-question\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge question\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"TMUA 2017 · Paper 1 · Question 11 — Determine the hundredth term without iterating a hundred times.\">",
  "options": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D",
    "E"
  ],
  "correct": "A",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Use the rule, not the first impression",
      "body": "<p>The first terms 7, 3, 1 do not by themselves establish a standard progression. Substitute the latest term into the given recurrence.</p>",
      "recap": "A recurrence, rather than the first few values, determines the next term.",
      "pitfall": "Do not invent an arithmetic or geometric pattern from three entries.",
      "pause": "Calculate the fourth term.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l09",
          "reminder": "Write enough terms to identify the rule before choosing a progression or a cycle."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Check that the sequence returns",
      "body": "<p>Continue applying the recurrence until a previous term reappears. Check each denominator along the way. Because the next term depends only on the current one, returning to a previous term forces the subsequent values to repeat.</p>",
      "recap": "A deterministic recurrence repeats its future when an earlier state returns.",
      "pitfall": "A visually similar group is not enough: verify the return using the recurrence.",
      "pause": "Calculate the next terms and identify the first complete repeating block.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l09",
          "reminder": "Returning to an earlier term in this one-term recurrence verifies a cycle."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Locate the position within a cycle",
      "body": "<p>Use the cycle length you found to separate the requested index into complete blocks and a remainder. Align the remainder with the position of the first given term.</p>",
      "recap": "Use the index modulo the cycle length.",
      "pitfall": "A remainder of zero selects the last term in a block, not its first term.",
      "pause": "Determine the cycle length, locate the requested term within a block and evaluate that term.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l09",
          "reminder": "Count complete cycles and then align the remaining position with the starting index."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>The rule gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>, so the cycle is <strong><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math></strong>. Every denominator used in this cycle is nonzero.</p><p>There are 25 complete four-term blocks in the first 100 terms. Term 100 is the last term of a block: <strong><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>, answer A.</strong></p>",
